<<~ ahu slot=`#/research-brief-wave-1-seat-b-never-lose-drafts-t`>>

! Research brief, wave 1, seat B: never-lose drafts, temp layers, and durable AI memory

You stand the whole Lares house if you know it; otherwise work plainly. Mask: `Draft-Keeper: Lares (Tasked Spirit)`. Use web search. Cite every claim with a URL. Write final.md in your seat dir (under 1800 words).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/local-context`>>

!! Local context (you cannot read the repo; these facts come from the controller's code reads)

The Lares stack: a local-first, web3-only "vessel" (Node daemon) that holds an Automerge CRDT store of tiddler-like records ("memes") composed into wikis by RECIPES over BAGS (a TiddlyWiki-like model). Layers top to bottom in a wiki: temp -> draft -> personal -> working -> canon -> kahua (governance) -> library bags -> oracle. Records stay sealed at rest under a custody key (passphrase and/or paper-pin slots). A disk PROJECTOR renders the latest text of some records to plaintext `.mem` files (memetic wikitext) on disk; projection holds latest text only, never CRDT history. An AI agent (Claude Code, Codex) reads and writes memory through an MCP server into the daemon's own wiki.

Layout today: one root ($LAR_ROOT, default ~/.lares; in the container ~/.lares/state mounts at /state) holding XDG-shaped subtrees: data/ (data/lares/{vessel,identity,nexus}, data/lararium/sensoriums/{memory,mesh,memetic-wikitext} with chroma.sqlite3 indexes), state/ (projection, harvest, harvest-stage), cache/ (scratch sensoriums), config/, run/ (sockets), plus bags/ and wikis/ projection folders for house-minted and private bags. The source checkout (git) holds bags/ and wikis/ for canon-sourced bags; those project back into git. Path names repeat semantics (".lares/state/data/lares/identity", ".lares/state/data/lares/vessel/st").

The operator re-founds the hearth often in early alpha (wipe the state root, found fresh keys and a fresh charter, re-seed canon from git). A code read found what a re-found loses:
* Plaintext that projects only under the state root (the daemon wiki where AI memory lands, house-minted bags, private planes, per-face kahua folders) dies with it.
* The temp, personal and (by default) draft layers never project at all.
* All CRDT history (heads, versions, concurrent drafts, signed governance acts) and every key/identity (custody, seat keys, the Nexus AID).
* Saves held in memory while custody sat locked.
The memory index (vector sensorium) rebuilds from source transcripts that survive elsewhere.

The operator's direction: (1) the immediate need: essential user data and the AI memory stage reach disk as plaintext projections, even temp layers (behind a flag, with an automated, principled cleanup); (2) a root layout inside ~/.lares split by what survives and how, so a couple of commands bundle/export a backup and re-import it after a re-found. Identity continuity across re-founds, face bundles, emergency recovery and backup restores stay DEFERRED until after a two-operator Nexus stands; note them, don't design them deep.

House laws that bind any proposal: web3-only and local-first (no cloud service in the stack proper; a web2 bridge sits behind a boundary); no credential through a flag, env var or pipe; plaintext on disk carries cleartext only by deliberate projection (the operator owns that choice per bag); a projection stays a deliberate act, not a side-channel.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/your-question`>>

!! Your question

What golden principles, prior art and best practices govern (a) persisting drafts, temp and in-progress work so a crash, wipe or reset never loses it, while keeping it from piling up forever, and (b) making an AI agent's memory durable, inspectable and portable as plaintext?

Read at least: editor autosave/swap/recovery designs (Vim swap files, Emacs auto-save and backup files, VS Code hot exit, JetBrains local history); email and messaging draft persistence; Google Docs / Notion offline drafts; write-ahead logs and journaling as a recovery pattern; CRDT snapshot/compaction and how Automerge and Yjs export a doc's history or a plain snapshot; soft delete, trash and TTL retention as cleanup patterns (macOS/Windows trash, Gmail drafts/trash, S3 lifecycle, tombstone GC in CRDTs); "plain text as the durable layer" arguments; AI agent memory systems and their persistence formats (Claude Code memory files and CLAUDE.md, the MCP reference memory server, Letta/MemGPT, mem0, Zep, LangGraph checkpointers, Cursor rules/memories), especially which store memory as human-readable files vs opaque DBs and why.

Deliver:
# Five to nine principles, each one sentence plus its sources.
# A design pattern for projecting the temp/draft/personal layers to plaintext behind a flag: where files land, naming, when they flush (on write, debounced, on idle, on shutdown), how they re-ingest, and a cleanup procedure (TTL, promote-or-expire, trash with a retention window, a size cap) that runs automatically and leaves an audit trail.
# A design pattern for the AI memory stage: plaintext-first, one file per memory, an index that rebuilds, and what prior art says about conflicts, dedupe and forgetting.
# Risks: plaintext of private layers on disk (threat model, disk encryption, per-bag opt-in), and what prior art does about it.
# Anything in prior art that contradicts the operator's direction; name it plainly.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

